Z||000000||Philphine||01-01-2001||04:30 PM||||<BR>you know how on the gt facias front and rear, even though they're one piece, they make it look like the ground effects are added on? i guess thats to make it match up to what has been added to the body on the sides. is there any way to fill that seam in and make it look smooth? since i have the integrated rocker panels on my '87 and nothing on my '84 (found rear gt facias for both), both with no side seams, i think it would look better.<P>a couple of times i've noticed theogre mention plastic welding. what exactly is that? similar to hot glue maybe? could i lay a bead down that seam and sand it down smooth? think fiber glass would bond to it and flex well enough? thanks for any thoughs or ideas.   <P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe (cheap thrill)||||reg||1
Z||000001||MikeE||01-02-2001||08:02 AM||||I noticed that my local NAPA store had a repair kit specifically for urethane bumpers.  I read the package and it said the filler remains flexible when dry but can still be sanded.  Since this filler was meant for our facia material I would this it is the best way to go but these repair kits might not have enough filler for what you plan to do.  However, you may be able to find out what it is and order it in a larger quantity.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||01-23-2001||07:07 PM||||i've got my rear facia at work. got the seam area sanded down to bare plastic. what they gave me at napa is seam sealer. the only reason i haven't applied it yet is i can't find the caulking gun. i may go get one at lunch. if anyone thinks this might not be the right stuff please say so. i may try to check here again if i get one at lunch. otherwise i'll wait and do it at home or later one day at work. any thoughts appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||01-24-2001||12:01 AM||||I don't believe that is what you want to use! All the bumper repair stuff that I've ever purchased has been a two part material, mix it like epoxy.<BR>The stuff they sold you is used to seal body seams like wher the trunk floor pan meets the vertical rear pan in a common automobile trunk.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||01-24-2001||12:08 AM||||too late. got a caulk gun at lunch and put it on. guess i have to wait for it to set up and see what i get. if i try this again ind. resto guy, what do you call what you're talking about?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||01-24-2001||10:58 AM||||well, i don't think it's going to set up firm enough. i'll wait a few days to be sure and in the mean time try to find out what to really use. back to the drawing board. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||01-24-2001||12:04 PM||||just in case someone else wants to know (and not because i like constantly replying to my own threads) i asked the same question on the corvette forum (i figure they've been dealing with urethane bumpers longer) and was told to use Duramix #4030 urethane filler.<P>one of the guys that answered posted a fiero pic with his answer. i mentioned the forum so if it's something he owns maybe he'll wander in. nothing against the mr2 guys, but if there was a link developed with another forum, i rather it was a gm one.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||01-24-2001||03:54 PM||||I use a product from Evercoat, Plastik Works. Part # 237 (?), it's a flexible bumper patch that 'sets up quickly & dries fast, sands easily to excellent feather edge, & resists solvents and temperature extremes.<BR>It's good stuff! I am quite sure that I purchased it at Car Quest. I also use some compareable stuff for aircraft applications if it's a large repair.<BR>If you can't find it in your area, let me know and I'll pick up a kit for you.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||01-24-2001||04:39 PM||||Be sure to follow the directions carefully. Some of thsoe type products have limitations on how thick they can be applied.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||01-24-2001||11:07 PM||||already got something. it's a 3m product, don't have it in front of me right now. went on ok. just did a little sanding to see how it would look. if it holds i think i'll like it. since i couldn't find an aftermarket rear facia this will be just different enough. maybe. still wish i could have found out who makes those exhaust spot fill ins.||||reg||1
